course: dictionary
lesson: 4313
title: Old words, new meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to a certain time. «Groovy» is slang, and it is dated, which means people used it mostly in the past. In the 1960s and 1970s it meant cool, fashionable or very nice. Context helps you spot this: Grandad says, «That's groovy!» He is talking about something he liked, so groovy means great.
explain: Dictionaries mark words with labels. Slang is informal talk that people use with friends. Dated means a word sounds old-fashioned now. A label warns you that a word may sound funny if you use it today, so think about who you are talking to.
explain: Groovy has a second meaning, but it is rare. It can mean having grooves, which are lines cut into a surface. Read the sentence around the word to decide which meaning fits.
fact: «Groovy» means cool, fashionable, exciting or very nice.
fact: «Groovy» was popular in the 1960s and 1970s.
fact: Today «groovy» often sounds funny or old-fashioned.
fact: «Groovy» can also mean having grooves, which are lines cut into a surface, but this meaning is rare.
fact: Mum's word «cool» is an old jazz word from the 1930s and 1940s that never died.
exercise: Grandad says, «That's groovy!» What does groovy mean here? | Covered in grooves | *Really cool or very nice | Wet and grey | Sad and tired
exercise: Why might a modern child sound funny saying «groovy»? | Because it means something very sad | *Because the word sounds old-fashioned now | Because a word can be dated, so it sounds old-fashioned now | Because it is only a Scottish word
exercise: «The old record had deep groovy lines cut across it.» What does groovy mean here? | *Having grooves, lines cut into a surface | Very tasty | Fashionable and exciting | Very cool
test: Which label tells you a word sounds old-fashioned now? | *Dated | Plural | Scots | Rare
test: Mia says «That's bussin'!» and Grandad says «That's groovy!». Which one is the dated word? | Both are dated | *Grandad's groovy | Mia's bussin' | Neither is slang
exam: Which fact about groovy is correct? | It means sad and tired | It is a word from the 1930s jazz age | *It means cool and was popular in the 1960s and 1970s | It means a grey, wet day
